# Daily news passages, 11 Nov 2026 (English). Written in our own words from the day's headlines.
EN = {
"E": [
("National Education Day", "India marks National Education Day on the eleventh of November every year. The day is kept in honour of Maulana Abul Kalam Azad, who was the first Education Minister of free India. He believed that a nation can grow only when each child goes to school. He worked hard to open new schools and colleges and to take learning to villages. On this day schools and colleges hold talks, essay writing and quiz events. Teachers tell students about his life and his love for books. The day reminds us that learning does not end with a degree. A person who reads a little each day keeps growing all through life. Parents also have a big role, because the first lessons are learnt at home. If we want a strong country, we must give every boy and girl a fair chance to study. Good schools, kind teachers and regular practice can change the future of a child. Let us use this day to thank our teachers and to promise that we will never stop learning."),
("Bank Holiday on Bhai Dooj", "Banks are closed in some states today because of Bhai Dooj and other local festivals. The holiday is not for the whole country. Branches are shut only in a few states, such as Uttar Pradesh, West Bengal, Sikkim, Manipur and Himachal Pradesh. In other places banks will work as on any normal day. People who have urgent work should check the holiday list of their own city before they go to the branch. Even when a branch is closed, most services stay open. You can take out cash from a cash machine, send money with your phone and pay bills from home. Net banking works day and night. Still, some jobs need a visit to the branch, such as a new locker or a big loan. For such work it is wise to plan ahead. Bhai Dooj is the day when sisters pray for the long life of their brothers and brothers give them gifts. It comes two days after Diwali and brings the festival week to a happy close."),
("Rain and Snow in the Hills", "The weather office has said that a fresh western disturbance is on its way to the north of the country. Because of it, parts of the Kashmir valley may get heavy rain and snow in the next two days. Jammu and Kashmir and Ladakh may also see strong winds. People living in the hills have been told to stay alert and to avoid travel on high roads when the snow is heavy. Farmers should keep their cut crop in a safe place. In the plains the days will stay dry, but the nights will turn colder. Light fog may be seen early in the morning in some parts of Uttar Pradesh. Drivers should keep their speed low and use fog lights. Winter is now at the door, so it is time to take out warm clothes. Old people and small children need more care in this season. A warm meal, clean water and a short walk in the sun can keep many small health problems away."),
("Third One Day Match Washed Out", "The third one day match between India and New Zealand could not be played because of rain. The toss was held on time, but then the rain came down and did not stop. The ground staff tried their best with covers and machines, yet the field stayed wet. In the end the match was called off and not a single ball was bowled. Fans who had come from far away went home sad. India had won the first two games, so the team still leads the series of five matches by two to nil. One more win will give India the series. New Zealand can now only hope to draw level. Rain is a part of cricket and no team can fight it. Players use such breaks to rest, to plan and to work on small faults. The next match will be very important for both sides. Lovers of the game will pray for a clear sky and a full day of good cricket."),
("A Beach in Goa Wins Praise", "Palolem beach in the south of Goa has been named the best beach in Asia in a new travel list. The same list puts it at the sixth place in the world. This is a proud moment for the state and for the whole country. Palolem is known for its calm blue water, white sand and long line of palm trees. The sea here is not very deep near the shore, so families feel safe. Small boats take visitors out to see dolphins early in the morning. Local people run small huts and food stalls that serve fresh fish and rice. Such praise brings more visitors, and more visitors bring more work for local youth. But it also brings a duty. A beach stays lovely only when it stays clean. Visitors should not throw plastic or bottles on the sand. If guests and hosts both take care, this small beach will keep its charm for many years to come."),
("Festive Rush on the Rail Site", "Many people faced slow login on the rail ticket site during the festival week. Some users said on social media that the site had stopped working. The rail ticket company has said that there was no break in service. It said that the delay was only because a very large number of people were trying to book at the same time. Diwali, Bhai Dooj and Chhath come close to each other, so lakhs of people travel home in these days. Trains are full and tickets are hard to get. Railways run many special trains in this season to help the crowd. Passengers should book early and keep their user name and password ready. It is also wise to try at a time when the load is low. Never give money to an unknown agent who promises a sure seat. Travel light, reach the station early and keep your ticket and an identity card with you. A little care makes the long trip home safe and easy."),
("Boat Ride on the Yamuna", "A new boat ride has started on the river Yamuna in Delhi. It has been named the Namo Yamuna River Safari. The ride begins at Sonia Vihar and goes up to Jagatpur and back. The full round trip is about six kilometres long. A Union Minister and the Chief Minister of Delhi were present when the first boat set off. The aim is to bring people closer to the river and to help tourism in the city. For many years people in Delhi have seen the Yamuna only from a bridge. Now they can sit in a boat and watch birds and the open sky. Such a ride can also make people think about the health of the river. A river is not a drain. It gives us water, food and life. If each home and each factory stops sending dirty water into it, the Yamuna can be clean once again. The new ride is a small step that may help in this big work."),
("Awards for Clean Cities", "The President of India is to give away the awards of the yearly clean city survey at a function in Vigyan Bhavan in New Delhi today. The survey looks at how clean our towns and cities are. Teams visit the streets, markets, public toilets and waste plants. They also ask the people of the city what they think. Marks are given for door to door waste pick up, for clean drains and for the way waste is turned into useful things. Cities that do well get a prize and a place of pride. This healthy race has changed many towns in the last few years. Streets that were full of waste are now neat. But a city cannot stay clean with the work of the staff alone. Each one of us has to help. We must keep wet and dry waste apart and must not throw it on the road. A clean city means less illness and a happy life for all."),
("Poor Air in Delhi After Diwali", "The air in Delhi and nearby cities has stayed very poor after Diwali. Many air stations in the city gave a reading of more than three hundred and fifty on Tuesday. Places like Pusa and Chandni Chowk were among the worst. Doctors say that such air can harm even a healthy person. It hurts the eyes, the throat and the lungs. Children, old people and those who have asthma are at the most risk. Smoke from crackers is only one cause. Dust from roads, smoke from cars and the burning of waste add to the load. Cold air and slow wind keep the dirt close to the ground. People should avoid a morning walk on such days and should wear a good mask when they go out. Car pools and public buses can cut down the smoke. Clean air is not the job of one office. It needs the help of each home, each driver and each farmer."),
("Free Gas Cylinder Help in Delhi", "The Delhi government has said that it will send money for free cooking gas cylinders to about fifteen lakh families. The help will go straight into the bank account of the family. This will give relief to poor homes at a time when the cost of living is high. Cooking gas is clean and safe when we compare it with wood or coal. Smoke from a wood fire harms the eyes and lungs of women who cook for long hours. A gas stove saves time, so women can use the free hours for study or work. For such a plan to work well, the list of families must be correct and the bank account must be linked. People should keep their papers ready and should not pay any agent for this help. They should also use gas with care. Turn off the knob after cooking and check the pipe from time to time. Small habits can stop big accidents at home."),
],
"M": [
("UPSC Mains Result and the Next Step", "The Union Public Service Commission declared the result of the Civil Services Main Examination 2026 on 6 November. In all, 2,526 candidates have qualified for the Personality Test, which is the last stage of the selection. These candidates now have to fill the Detailed Application Form between 11 November and 25 November. The dates of the interview will be announced later. The Civil Services Examination is held in three stages: Preliminary, Main and Personality Test. Lakhs of young people apply every year, but only a small number reach the final list. The interview does not test bookish knowledge alone. The board looks for honesty, balance of mind, clear thinking and interest in public affairs. Candidates should read a good newspaper daily, know their own state and district well, and practise speaking in simple, clear language. Those who could not qualify this time should not lose heart. Many toppers succeeded only in their third or fourth attempt. Steady work, a calm mind and regular revision are the real keys to success."),
("ICSE Class 10 Date Sheet Released", "The Council for the Indian School Certificate Examinations has released the date sheet for the Class 10 board examination of 2027. According to the schedule, the papers will begin on 15 February and end on 31 March 2027. About 2.70 lakh students are expected to appear. The English paper has been placed on the very first day. Students have also been told to reach the examination centre earlier than in past years. The Class 12 examination of the same board will begin on 9 February 2027. With the dates in hand, students now have nearly three months to prepare. Teachers advise that a simple timetable should be made at once. Difficult subjects should be given the fresh hours of the morning. Old question papers must be solved within the fixed time, because speed and neat writing both carry value. Proper sleep, light food and a short break after every hour of study keep the mind fresh. Parents should encourage their children and avoid comparing them with others."),
("JEE Main 2027 Registrations Cross 5.72 Lakh", "More than 5.72 lakh candidates have already registered for the Joint Entrance Examination (Main) 2027, according to reports. The last date to apply is 22 November. JEE Main is the gateway to the National Institutes of Technology and many other engineering colleges of the country. It is also the first step for those who wish to sit for JEE Advanced and enter the Indian Institutes of Technology. Students are advised not to wait for the last day, because heavy traffic on the website often causes delay. Before filling the form, they should keep a recent photograph, signature, Class 10 certificate and a valid identity card ready. Every detail, such as name, date of birth and category, must match the certificates. A small mistake can create trouble at the time of admission. Along with the form, preparation must go on. Physics, Chemistry and Mathematics need daily practice. Mock tests on a computer help a student to manage time and to stay calm on the day of the real examination."),
("India Beat UAE in Under-19 Asia Cup", "The Indian team returned to winning ways in the Under-19 Asia Cup 2026 by defeating the United Arab Emirates by 70 runs. The Group A match was played at the Al Amerat Cricket Ground. Batting first, India scored 309 runs for the loss of 7 wickets in 50 overs. The star of the day was Aaryavir Sehwag, son of former opener Virender Sehwag, who played a superb innings of 160 runs. In reply, the UAE batsmen fought well but were bowled out before reaching the target. India will now meet Afghanistan in the semi-final. The Under-19 level is the nursery of Indian cricket. Many great players, including Virat Kohli and Shubman Gill, first caught the eye of the nation in such tournaments. Young players learn here how to handle pressure, travel and the attention of the media. Coaches say that talent alone is not enough at this age. Fitness, discipline and respect for the game decide who will go on to play for the senior team."),
("Haj Quota for 2027", "India has received a quota of 1,75,025 pilgrims for Haj 2027 under the bilateral agreement signed with Saudi Arabia. The Union Minister of Minority Affairs held talks with Saudi officials in Jeddah on arrangements for the pilgrims. Haj is one of the largest yearly gatherings in the world, and people from more than 150 countries take part in it. For Indian pilgrims, the arrangements are made by the Haj Committee of India and by private tour operators. Flights, buses, tents, food and medical care for such a large number of people need months of planning. In recent years, many services have gone online. Applications, payment and selection by draw are now done through a website and a mobile application. Pilgrims are given training before the journey, so that they know the rules and the route. Since many of them are above 60 years of age, health camps and helpers are also provided. Good planning by both countries makes this long and difficult journey smooth and safe."),
("Booker Prize 2026", "British author M. John Harrison has won the Booker Prize 2026 for his novel, according to reports. At the age of 81, he has become the oldest winner in the history of the award. The prize carries a sum of 50,000 pounds. The Booker Prize is given every year to the best novel written in English and published in the United Kingdom or Ireland. It is counted among the most respected awards in the world of books. Indian readers know the prize well. Writers such as Arundhati Roy, Kiran Desai and Aravind Adiga have won it in the past. A big award does more than honour one writer. It brings new readers to serious books and helps publishers and bookshops. In an age of short videos, the habit of reading a full book is becoming rare. Yet a good novel teaches patience and helps us to understand people who are very different from us. Students who read for 30 minutes daily improve their language, memory and power of thought."),
("Draft Voter List in Uttarakhand", "The draft voter list of Uttarakhand will be published on 12 November. With this, a special summary revision of the electoral roll will begin in the state. Claims and objections will be accepted from 12 November to 11 December. The qualifying date has been fixed as 1 January 2027. It means that a citizen who completes 18 years of age on or before that date can apply to be included in the list. The voter list is the foundation of a fair election. If the name of an eligible person is missing, he or she loses the right to vote. If the name of a dead or shifted person remains, the list becomes faulty. Therefore, every citizen should check the draft list carefully. Forms for adding a new name, for correction and for removal can be filled online or given to the booth level officer. Political parties also appoint their agents to help in this work. An accurate list protects the value of every single vote."),
("Complaints to the Women's Commission", "According to a report published today, the women's commission is receiving about 410 complaints every day. The yearly figure has crossed 1.5 lakh. The report says that women no longer remain silent when they face cruelty. This change is important. Earlier, many women did not complain because of fear, shame or pressure from the family. Now education, mobile phones and helplines have given them courage. A complaint can be sent online, by post or through a helpline number. Most cases are related to violence at home, dowry, harassment at the workplace and cyber crime. Experts say that a rise in complaints does not always mean a rise in crime. Often it shows that trust in the system has grown. However, registering a complaint is only the first step. Quick inquiry, legal help and counselling must follow. Police stations need more women officers, and courts need to decide such cases faster. A society becomes truly safe only when its men also learn to respect women."),
("Crude Oil Falls but Pump Prices Stay", "The price of Brent crude oil has fallen in the world market in recent days, according to reports. Yet the price of petrol and diesel at Indian pumps has not come down, and people are asking the government why. India buys more than 80 per cent of its crude oil from other countries. So, when crude becomes cheaper, the import bill falls. But the price at the pump depends on many other things as well. Central excise duty, state VAT, the commission of the dealer and the cost of refining and transport are all added to it. The value of the rupee against the dollar also matters. Oil companies sometimes use a period of low prices to recover their earlier losses. High fuel prices affect everyone. They raise the cost of transport, and then the price of vegetables, milk and other goods also goes up. Economists suggest that taxes on fuel should be reviewed from time to time, so that the benefit reaches the common man."),
("Bihar Raises Dearness Allowance", "The Bihar government has raised the dearness allowance of its employees by 3 per cent before Chhath Puja. The arrears will also be paid. In another meeting, the state cabinet took 31 decisions, including a plan for a water metro in Patna. Dearness allowance, or DA, is paid to government employees and pensioners to reduce the effect of rising prices. It is revised from time to time on the basis of the consumer price index. When DA goes up, lakhs of families get more money to spend, and local markets also gain. On the other hand, the burden on the state budget increases. A water metro is a system of modern boats which carry passengers on a river, just as a metro train does on land. Patna lies on the bank of the Ganga, so such a service can reduce the load on its crowded roads. Kochi in Kerala already runs a water metro. If the plan is carried out well, travel in Patna will become cheaper, faster and cleaner."),
],
"H": [
("Price Cut on Cancer Medicines", "Revised maximum retail prices of several anti-cancer medicines came into effect on 9 November under the \"trade margin rationalisation\" policy. According to reports, the National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority (NPPA) has covered 137 non-scheduled drugs, and prices of more than 1,000 formulations have been reduced. Consumers are expected to save over Rs 3,500 crore annually. One breast cancer medicine, for instance, is now reported to cost Rs 37,358.35 instead of Rs 83,520. Under this policy, the margin that distributors, stockists and retailers may add to the manufacturer's price is capped at a fixed percentage; consequently, the final price falls without any loss in quality. Cancer treatment is prolonged and expensive: surgery, chemotherapy, radiation and follow-up tests can exhaust the lifetime savings of a middle-class family. Cheaper medicines will, therefore, provide substantial relief. Experts, however, emphasise that affordability is only one side of the problem. Early detection, awareness about tobacco, and specialised hospitals in smaller towns are equally indispensable."),
("Deemed Suspension After 48 Hours in Custody", "The Supreme Court has observed that a government employee who remains in judicial custody for more than 48 hours is deemed to be suspended automatically; no separate order is required for this purpose. The observation was made by a two-judge Bench while hearing a bribery case concerning a hospital manager in Bihar. The principle is already contained in the service rules of the Central and State Governments. Its rationale is straightforward: a public servant who is behind bars cannot discharge official duties, and the dignity of the office must be protected until the investigation is concluded. \"Suspension\", it must be remembered, is not a punishment. It is a temporary measure, during which the employee receives a subsistence allowance (normally 50 per cent of the basic pay). If the charge is ultimately not proved, the period is regularised and full salary is restored. For candidates preparing for clerical and assistant-grade posts, such rulings are significant, since questions on conduct rules, disciplinary proceedings and service jurisprudence frequently appear in departmental examinations."),
("Grandson's Right in Partitioned Property", "In a significant ruling reported today, the Supreme Court has held that property which a father receives through partition is to be treated as his separate (self-acquired) property; consequently, a grandson cannot claim a birthright in it. The distinction between \"ancestral\" and \"self-acquired\" property lies at the heart of Hindu succession law. In ancestral or coparcenary property, a child acquires an interest by birth; in self-acquired property, the owner enjoys absolute freedom to sell, gift or bequeath it. Since the Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act, 2005, daughters have been recognised as coparceners with rights equal to those of sons. Family property disputes constitute a considerable proportion of civil litigation in India, and many of them continue for 20 or 30 years. Legal experts, therefore, recommend three precautions: (1) partition should be recorded through a registered deed; (2) revenue records must be mutated promptly; and (3) a clear, properly attested will should be prepared. Such documentation prevents bitterness among relatives and saves precious time of the courts."),
("India on the Strait of Hormuz", "On 10 November, the spokesperson of the Ministry of External Affairs (MEA) stated that India supports safe and unimpeded passage of ships through the Strait of Hormuz. He added that New Delhi is in contact with all the parties concerned in order to protect Indian-flagged vessels and Indian seafarers. The Strait of Hormuz, situated between Iran and Oman, connects the Persian Gulf with the Gulf of Oman and the Arabian Sea. At its narrowest point it is only about 33 kilometres wide; nevertheless, roughly one-fifth of the world's petroleum passes through it. For India, which imports the greater part of its crude oil and liquefied natural gas from West Asia, the route is of vital strategic importance. Any disturbance there immediately pushes up freight and insurance charges and, eventually, domestic fuel prices. Moreover, lakhs of Indians are employed in the Gulf countries, and thousands serve in the merchant navy. India's consistent position, therefore, is that disputes must be resolved through dialogue and that international sea lanes must remain open to all."),
("Modi-Zelensky Conversation", "Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky spoke to Prime Minister Narendra Modi over the telephone ahead of a fresh round of US-backed Russia-Ukraine negotiations in Abu Dhabi. According to reports, the two leaders discussed India's possible contribution to diplomatic efforts as well as the restoration of grain-export routes through the Black Sea. India reiterated its willingness to support a peaceful resolution of the conflict. Separately, the External Affairs Minister is reported to have discussed the situation with his Russian counterpart. Since February 2022, India has followed a carefully balanced policy: it has maintained its long-standing partnership with Moscow, provided humanitarian assistance to Kyiv, and repeatedly declared that \"this is not an era of war\". The conflict has affected the entire world. Prices of wheat, sunflower oil, fertiliser and energy rose sharply, and developing countries suffered the most. Analysts believe that nations which enjoy the confidence of both sides can facilitate communication, although a durable settlement will ultimately depend upon the belligerents themselves."),
("Why AQI Figures Differ", "The Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) has clarified why its Air Quality Index (AQI) figures differ from those displayed by private mobile applications, and has denied any under-reporting. The explanation is essentially technical. The official Indian index is calculated from 24-hour average concentrations of pollutants such as PM2.5, PM10, nitrogen dioxide and ozone; its scale ends at 500. Many private applications, on the other hand, follow the United States standard, use hourly or real-time readings, and frequently depend on low-cost sensors. Consequently, the same air may be shown as 380 on one platform and above 600 on another. Neither figure is necessarily \"false\"; they simply measure different things. Nevertheless, the confusion undermines public confidence. Experts suggest that (a) the number of certified monitoring stations should be increased, (b) raw data should be published transparently, and (c) citizens should be educated about the method of calculation. Accurate measurement is the foundation of effective policy; without it, neither health advisories nor emergency restrictions can be properly designed."),
("India-Oman Naval Exercise", "The Indian Navy's guided-missile destroyer INS Surat has arrived in Oman to participate in the 15th edition of the India-Oman bilateral naval exercise, according to reports. INS Surat, commissioned in January 2025, is the fourth and final ship of the Visakhapatnam class (Project 15B) and has been built indigenously at Mazagon Dock, Mumbai. Oman is India's closest defence partner in the Gulf region; it is the only country there with which all three Indian services - Army, Navy and Air Force - conduct regular joint exercises. Such exercises generally comprise two phases: a harbour phase, involving professional discussions and planning conferences, and a sea phase, which includes manoeuvres, anti-piracy drills, helicopter operations and communication procedures. Their objective is \"interoperability\", that is, the ability of two navies to operate together during a crisis. Since nearly 90 per cent of India's trade by volume moves by sea, the security of the Arabian Sea and the western Indian Ocean is indispensable for the national economy."),
("Electric Cars for the Government Fleet", "According to a report published today, petrol and diesel cars are to be phased out of the government fleet, and electric vehicles (EVs) costing up to Rs 20 lakh will henceforth be given preference; officers are also to be provided with laptops. If implemented systematically, the decision could yield several advantages. Firstly, the running cost of an electric car is approximately one-fourth that of a conventional vehicle. Secondly, EVs produce no tail-pipe emissions - a crucial consideration when air quality in northern cities deteriorates every winter. Thirdly, bulk purchases by government departments encourage manufacturers to expand production, which gradually reduces prices for ordinary buyers. The transition, however, is not without difficulties. Charging stations are still inadequate; batteries are expensive and largely imported; and safe disposal of used batteries requires a well-regulated recycling industry. Furthermore, an EV is genuinely \"clean\" only when the electricity it consumes is generated from renewable sources. Policy-makers must, therefore, promote solar and wind power simultaneously."),
("United States, Iran and the Road to Talks", "The President of the United States has stated that the military confrontation with Iran is \"effectively over\" and that Washington is not planning major new strikes at present, according to reports. Iran's President, for his part, has declared that Tehran is prepared to negotiate on equal terms but will not yield to pressure; Tehran is also reported to have put forward seven conditions. Differences over the nuclear programme, economic sanctions and naval movements in the Gulf nevertheless persist, and no definite agreement has yet been concluded. For India, stability in West Asia is not a distant diplomatic question; it is an economic necessity. Approximately 90 lakh Indians live and work in the Gulf region, and their remittances support millions of households. Energy supplies, shipping routes and trade corridors are likewise involved. History demonstrates that cease-fires are fragile unless they are followed by patient, verifiable negotiations. The international community, consequently, expects restraint from every side and a sincere commitment to dialogue."),
("A Busy Day for Indian Diplomacy", "The past 24 hours have been unusually eventful for Indian diplomacy. According to reports, India and Lebanon held their first-ever Foreign Office Consultations; the Ministry of External Affairs extended an invitation to the Prime Minister of Nepal to visit India; and bilateral discussions on customs and trade facilitation were conducted with the United States in New Delhi. Each engagement illustrates a different dimension of foreign policy. \"Foreign Office Consultations\" are structured meetings between senior officials, in which the entire relationship - political, commercial, cultural and consular - is reviewed. Relations with Nepal rest upon an open border of nearly 1,750 kilometres, shared rivers and deep-rooted cultural ties; high-level visits help to resolve irritants concerning trade, transit and hydro-electric projects. Negotiations on trade facilitation, meanwhile, aim at simplifying documentation, reducing clearance time at ports and harmonising standards. Modern diplomacy, evidently, is no longer confined to treaties and summits: it directly influences employment, prices and the everyday convenience of citizens."),
],
}
